<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vatican Library</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1475">1475</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Vatican Apostolic Library</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Vatican Library is the library of the Holy See in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vatican-city">Vatican City</a>, formally established in 1475 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-sixtus-iv">Pope Sixtus IV</a>, building on the collection of Nicholas V. It holds one of the world's most important collections of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>, including the Codex Vaticanus of the Bible. In 1623 much of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bibliotheca-palatina">Bibliotheca Palatina</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a> was added to it as war booty.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vatican-library.jpg" alt="Vatican Library" width="320" height="380" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Melozzo da Forlì · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Melozzo_da_Forl%C3%AC_001.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Scholars have traditionally divided the library's history into five periods: Pre-Lateran, Lateran, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avignon">Avignon</a>, Pre-Vatican and Vatican. At the Lateran Palace it grew to one of the most notable collections of illuminated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, until the palace was burnt and the collection plundered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-iv-of-france">Philip IV of France</a> in 1303. The Avignon popes added greatly to it, but between about 1370 and 1447 it was scattered, with parts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, Avignon and elsewhere. Pope Nicholas V planned a public library to make Rome a place of humanist scholarship. He combined some 350 Greek, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> codices from his predecessors with his own acquisitions. By 1455 it held 1,200 books, 400 of them in Greek.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Nicholas died in 1455, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-sixtus-iv">Pope Sixtus IV</a> established what is now known as the Vatican Library. Around 1481 it was the largest collection of books in the Western world. Around 1587 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-sixtus-v">Pope Sixtus V</a> commissioned the architect Domenico Fontana to build the new building that is still used today. In 1623 Maximilian I, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, gave the Palatine Library of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a>, about 3,500 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> that he had just taken as loot in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>. The manuscripts of the Dukes of Urbino arrived in 1657, and Queen Christina of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>'s library was bought in 1689.</p><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation</a> access was limited after the Index of banned books was introduced, particularly for Protestant scholars; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-xiii">Pope Leo XIII</a> formally reopened the library to scholars in 1883. In 1809 Napoleon Bonaparte had the contents seized and taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, and they were returned in 1817. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-xi">Pope Pius XI</a> the library was reorganised between the two world wars, and Father Franz Ehrle set up its first modern catalogue between 1927 and 1939.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The library was founded primarily as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> library, and holds 75,000 codices, 1.1 million printed books including some 8,500 incunabula, and 330,000 coins and medals. Its oldest documents date from the first century. Although it has always included Bibles, canon law and theology, it specialised from the beginning in secular books, and its collection of Greek and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> classics stood at the centre of the revival of classical culture during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>. It is open to anyone who can document their qualifications and research needs.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vatican Library” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Much of Heidelberg's great library, the Bibliotheca Palatina, was sent to Rome in 1623 as a gift to the pope after the city fell, and it remains in the <b>Vatican Library</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electoral-palatinate">Electoral Palatinate · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>…Baroque popes, Rome became the artistic capital of Europe: Sixtus IV built the Sistine Chapel and founded the <b>Vatican Library</b>, Julius II commissioned Michelangelo’s ceiling of the Sistine Chapel and Raphael’s frescoes in the Vatican, and the new basilica of Saint Peter, begun in 1506, was completed by…</blockquote><a href="/empire/papal-states">Papal States · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Patrons made the movement possible: the Medici in Florence, Federico da Montefeltro at Urbino, Alfonso V at Naples, Pope Nicholas V, who commissioned Latin translations of the Greek historians and gathered the books from which the <b>Vatican Library</b> grew, and Pope Pius II, himself a humanist writer.</blockquote><a href="/movement/renaissance-humanism">Renaissance humanism · People</a></li><li><blockquote>…brought some five hundred workers from Alexandria and Jerusalem; Monti della Corte (1940) found the translation almost completely wrong, and with the help of Arnold Van Lantschoot at the <b>Vatican Library</b> its three texts, in Coptic, Arabic and Geʽez, were shown to make no mention of foreign builders.</blockquote><a href="/work/rock-hewn-churches-of-lalibela">Rock-hewn churches of Lalibela · Reception</a></li><li><blockquote>Vatican City supports the worldwide government of the Holy See, with Vatican Radio founded in 1931, the Vatican Museums and the <b>Apostolic Library</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/vatican-city">Vatican City</a></li></ul></section>
